{"kind":"artifact","text":"Two closed-environment claims carry their qualification where they are made. Exact clean pushed head fc99f10d2d6aa5050b573d08e0a24de79648e09f on request/repo-remote-link; tree f0d7072c, checkout clean, origin matching. Base: merge-base with main is c679fb73; main is 3a5d952c, both verified at filing. Under codex's recut request 17620ad6 and my promise 9b07a473. This artifact rests on that promise and on its own stable predecessor at 5a4ee9e9, not on any sibling candidate, so retiring or staling one path cannot stale the rest.\n\nTHE THREAT MODEL IS SETTLED. Hugh ruled at 57df8d25 that the operator's ambient Git environment — the routing family, the GIT_CONFIG family, HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME — is outside gitseq's threat model: gitseq trusts the environment of the operator who runs it, and defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. The closed child-process behaviour in this lane is a determinism property, not a defence: the same repository answers regardless of the invoking shell. Those are different claims about one mechanism and only the first is one this project makes.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE IS. gitStatedEnvironment at app.go:214 and the closed-world test at repo_remote_test.go:711 both opened absolutely and narrowed to Unix several paragraphs later; standing alone each was false, because Go's os/exec adds SYSTEMROOT to what a Windows child receives. Both now carry the qualification in the opening sentence. Line numbers re-derived at this head. The tree is byte-identical to the two withdrawn commits carrying this change, so only provenance differs.\n\nWHY THE COMMIT WAS MADE THREE TIMES. The first rested on a child that had been retired; the second on a recovery chain that had gone stale. A commit trailer cannot be corrected after the fact the way a durable record can, so each time the commit had to be remade rather than amended. This one rests on a request that is live at filing and carries no dead-basis override anywhere in its chain.\n\nTHE SWEEP. The rest of internal/app and its tests carry no other absolute closed-environment claim.\n\nNO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES from the state reviewed at 5a4ee9e9.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux.","body":{"commit":"fc99f10d2d6aa5050b573d08e0a24de79648e09f","path":"internal/app"}}